{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And his impurity is upon him.</b> Rashi explains: the warning for an impure person who eats that which is pure is not explicitly stated in the Torah, but the Sages derived it by means of a gezerah shavah. Three karetot, etc., as taught in the first chapter of Shavuot — Rabbi Abbahu said: three karetot in shelamim. Why? Rashi explained: three karetot for an impure person who eats consecrated foods. One in the parashah of Emor: ״כל איש אשר יקרב מכל זרעכם אל הקדשים אשר יקדישו בני ישראל וטומאתו עליו ונכרתה״ etc. And we say in tractate Zevachim chapter 4: this יקרב is eating, and the reason it uses the language of הקרבה is that it speaks of something fit to be offered, such as an impure person who ate consecrated foods that were sanctified in a service vessel. And two of the karetot are here, in the parashah of Tzav: ״והנפש אשר תאכל מבשר זבח השלמים וטומאתו עליו ונכרתה״ etc. And adjacent to it: ״ונפש כי תגע בכל טמא ואכל מבשר זבח השלמים ונכרתה״ etc. One is a general rule — the one of ״אל הקדשים״ in the parashah of Emor, since it included all consecrated foods. And one is a particular — one of the two latter ones here in the parashah of Tzav. And this measure is expounded in Torat Kohanim at the beginning of the book, by the rule of ״a matter that was in the general rule and went out from the general rule״ etc. How so? In the parashah of Emor it is written ״והנפש אשר תאכל מבשר זבח השלמים״ — but from this verse, were shelamim not included in the general rule of all consecrated foods? And why did shelamim go out from the general rule, in the verse of the parashah of Tzav — ״והנפש אשר תאכל מבשר זבח השלמים״? To link the rest of the consecrated foods to them, to say: just as shelamim, which are distinctive in that they are consecrated foods of the altar and one is liable to karet on their account because of impurity, so too all consecrated foods of the altar — excluding consecrated foods of the Temple treasury, for which one is not liable to karet on account of impurity. <b>And if you ask:</b> why are these verses not judged by the rule of general and particular, to say: the general rule includes only what is in the particular, and from this you would learn: shelamim yes, other consecrated foods no, even though they are consecrated foods of the altar? <b>One may answer:</b> because the verses are distant from one another, and they are like two separate subjects. And so it is explicitly stated in Menachot, chapter ״כל המנחות באות מצה״, that where the particular is distant from the general rule, it is judged by the measure of ״a matter that was in the general rule״ etc. And one, a third explanation of these karetot: the extra one was written for things that are not eaten, such as the wood of the arrangement, frankincense, and incense — if an impure person ate them, he is liable to karet. Thus it is taught, and thus Rashi explained in the first chapter of Shavuot. And according to the plain sense, <b>and his impurity is upon him</b> — regarding impurity that goes out upon him from his own body, this is what the verse speaks of."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:40.249069347Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}